[0m$ [0mcd /home/agent/projects/netscript/wave7-billing-steer2 && git add -A && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'
Fix: the scaffold sweep renamed the fiction instead of removing it

The critique's Rule 8 item ("the gallery still shows scaffold strings") was
reported closed after the api-gateway / eu-west / v2.4.1 strings were deleted.
It was not closed. Deleting the cloud vocabulary removed the words someone had
already thought to name; the scaffold itself was still shipping behind words
nobody had:

  - a "Build pipeline" card in the billing gallery — "Twelve checks across
    lint, types, and tests. The slowest stage is the integration suite at 96
    seconds"
  - a "Deployment region" select whose options were billing periods
  - "P95 latency / 184ms / All regions" as the StatsGrid demo
  - Enable build cache · Auto-promote previews · Maintenance mode ·
    Locked by org policy · Service name · API key · Upload · Migration ·
    Deployment inspector · Region capacity · Loading deployments
  - three design-gallery page titles branded "NetScript design system", one
    describing "the scaffolded @netscript/fresh-ui theme"
  - the landing page's stat cards measuring the app itself: "UI source:
    app-owned", "Theme: paper & ink", "see RECORD.md"

Every one is replaced with Ledgerline vocabulary (dunning sequence, billing
period, collected/soft declines, plan name, webhook secret, invoice
inspector). The landing page's stat row is deleted rather than rewritten:
that layer receives no billing data, and a card that counts the app is a card
the customer cannot read — the lede and the route cards already do the job.

The denylist is what let this survive three passes under a green R8, so the
test now carries the terms plus two patterns for the shape: naming the UI
registry a component was copied from, and prose that points at the repository
record instead of the domain. The registry pattern caught a fourth site the
hand-sweep missed — the design gallery's own page descriptions.
EOF
